  "text": "Assistant: To find the value of the determinant:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nwe can use the property that the determinant of a matrix with symmetric elements can be simplified. Specifically, the determinant of a \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix with rows that are scalar multiples of each other is zero. However, in this case, we have a \\(3 \\times 3\\) matrix where each row is a scalar multiple of the row above it.\n\nLet's consider the matrix as a linear combination of two vectors, say \\(\\mathbf{v} = [\\cos \\alpha, \\cos \\beta, \\cos \\gamma]^T\\) and \\(\\mathbf{w} = [\\sin \\alpha, \\sin \\beta, \\sin \\gamma]^T\\). Then the given matrix can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n=\n\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v}\n\\cdot\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n1 & \\sin \\alpha & -\\cos \\alpha \\\\ \n1 & \\sin \\beta & -\\cos \\beta \\\\ \n1 & \\sin \\gamma & -\\cos \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe determinant of the \\(3 \\times 3\\) matrix is zero because its rows are linearly dependent (each row is constant and does not change with the angles). Therefore, the entire determinant simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v} \\cdot 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v}\\), which is the sum of the squares of the components of the vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v} = (\\cos \\alpha)^2 + (\\cos \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\gamma)^2\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1\\), we can write:\n\n\\[\n(\\cos \\alpha)^2 + (\\cos \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\gamma)^2 = 1 + 2((\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha) + (\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta) + (\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma))\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the fact that the angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\) are such that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), which implies a specific relationship between the sines and cosines of these angles. In particular, the sum of the products of the sines and cosines of these angles is zero:\n\n\\[\n(\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha) + (\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta) + (\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma) = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of the cosines is:\n\n\\[\n(\\cos \\alpha)^2 + (\\cos \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\gamma)^2 = 2((\\cos \\alpha)^2 + (\\cos \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\gamma)^2) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the determinant is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v} \\cdot 0 = 2 \\cdot 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of the determinant is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
